      body: `## A transformed society

In 146 BCE, Rome was the undisputed master of the Mediterranean. Yet within thirteen years, the Republic would see its first political murder in the streets of Rome, and within a century, it would collapse in civil war. What went wrong? The Romans themselves, as we saw in lesson 3, blamed **moral decline**, brought on by wealth and the absence of a rival. Modern historians have looked for deeper causes in the economic and social changes that the conquests brought to Italy.

## Slaves

The wars of conquest brought enormous numbers of **slaves** to Italy. Hundreds of thousands of captives were sold in the slave markets: 150,000 from Epirus in a single day in 167, 50,000 from Carthage in 146, and many more from Spain, Gaul, Sardinia, Greece, and Asia. The island of **Delos**, a free port under Roman protection after 166, became a great slave market, where, the geographer Strabo says, tens of thousands of slaves could be bought and sold in a day (14.5.2). Modern estimates, which are very uncertain, suggest that by the end of the Republic there were perhaps one to two million slaves in Italy, out of a total population of perhaps six to seven million; the figures are debated.

Slaves did every kind of work. Educated Greek slaves served as secretaries, doctors, teachers, and accountants in the households of the rich. Others worked in the mines, where conditions were terrible, or in the households of the urban poor. But the most important change was the use of slaves on large **agricultural estates**.

## The villa and the latifundia

With the profits of empire, the Roman elite bought up land in Italy. They developed large estates, worked by gangs of slaves, producing wine and olive oil for the market, or grazing sheep and cattle across the empty uplands of the south. The elder **Cato**, around 160 BCE, wrote a handbook, *On Agriculture*, for the owner of such an estate, a medium-sized **villa** of about a hundred **iugera** of vineyard or 240 of olives, worked by a small staff of slaves under a slave overseer, the **vilicus**. His advice was ruthlessly practical: "Sell worn-out oxen, … old wagons, old tools, an old slave, a sickly slave, and whatever else is superfluous" (2.7). The later Roman writer Pliny the Elder, in the first century CE, claimed that "the **latifundia**," huge estates, "have ruined Italy" (*Natural History* 18.35), though this is a later moralist's judgment.

## The small farmer

Meanwhile, the class that had formed the backbone of the Roman army, the **small farmers** who owned and worked their own land, seemed to be in trouble. The wars took men away from their farms for years at a time, in Spain, Greece, and Asia; some never returned, and others came home to find their farms ruined or in debt. Wealthy neighbors bought up or seized their land. Many drifted to Rome, swelling the urban poor, or became landless laborers. And because only men with property could serve in the legions, the decline of the small farmer threatened Rome's military manpower.

This is the picture given by our ancient sources, especially **Appian** (*Civil Wars* 1.7) and **Plutarch** (*Tiberius Gracchus* 8), and it was the picture that inspired the reforms of Tiberius Gracchus. Modern historians have questioned how accurate it is. The census figures, which are hard to interpret, do not show a sharp decline in the number of citizens. Archaeological surveys, such as the British School at Rome's survey of the area north of Rome, have found many small farms surviving through this period, though the pattern varied from region to region. And the evidence for large slave-run estates is strongest in some areas, such as Etruria, Campania, and the south, and weaker elsewhere. Some historians now argue that the problem was not a collapse of the peasantry, but a growing rural population without enough land, or a shortage of men who qualified for service. But the Romans of the time believed there was a crisis, and acted on that belief.

## Slave revolts

The concentration of slaves on great estates brought a new danger. In **Sicily**, where huge estates were worked by gangs of slaves, many recently enslaved free men from the east, two great slave revolts broke out: the **First Servile War** (c. 135–132 BCE), led by **Eunus**, a Syrian slave and wonder-worker who claimed to receive visions from the goddess Atargatis, and who proclaimed himself king, with tens of thousands of followers; and the **Second Servile War** (104–100 BCE). Both took years and full consular armies to suppress. The greatest slave revolt, that of **Spartacus** in Italy itself, came later, as we will see on page 6.

## The army

The army itself was changing. Wars in distant lands, especially the long and brutal wars in **Spain**, lasted years, brought little booty, and were deeply unpopular. In 151 and again in 138, the consuls conducting the levy were arrested by tribunes acting on behalf of citizens who refused to serve. The siege of **Numantia**, a Celtiberian town in northern Spain, dragged on until **133 BCE**, when it was finally captured and destroyed by **Scipio Aemilianus**, the destroyer of Carthage. Roman armies were also suffering defeats. In **137 BCE**, a consular army of some twenty thousand men was trapped near Numantia, and its commander, **Gaius Hostilius Mancinus**, made a treaty to save it, negotiated by his young quaestor, **Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus**. The senate repudiated the treaty and handed Mancinus over, naked and in chains, to the Numantines, who refused to accept him.

## Wealth and ambition

The empire also transformed the **elite**. The governors of provinces had opportunities for enormous enrichment, from booty, bribes, and extortion. The first permanent court of law in Rome, created in **149 BCE** by the **lex Calpurnia**, was a court for the recovery of money extorted by governors (*de repetundis*). Competition for office, which led to the provinces, became more expensive, as candidates spent fortunes on games and bribes. A new class of rich men outside the senate, the **equites**, "knights," businessmen who made fortunes from contracts for public works, army supplies, and tax collection, became increasingly important. Senators were forbidden by a law of 218 BCE, the **lex Claudia**, from owning large seagoing ships, and so from engaging openly in overseas trade, which left this field to the equites. The tensions between these groups would shape the politics of the next century.

> **Pause and consider.** Ancient writers blamed the crisis of the Republic on slaves, great estates, and the ruin of the small farmer; modern archaeologists have found many small farms surviving. Does it matter whether the crisis the Romans believed in was real, if they acted on their belief?`,
