      body: `## A noble reformer

**Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus** was born around **163 BCE** into the heart of the Roman nobility. His father, also named Tiberius, had been consul twice and censor. His mother, **Cornelia**, was the daughter of Scipio Africanus, the victor over Hannibal; widowed young, she refused an offer of marriage from the king of Egypt, Ptolemy VIII, and devoted herself to the education of her children, who were taught by Greek philosophers. A famous later story tells that when a woman visiting her showed off her jewels and asked to see Cornelia's, Cornelia brought in her sons and said, "**These are my jewels**" (Valerius Maximus 4.4 pr.). His sister **Sempronia** was married to Scipio Aemilianus.

Plutarch reports that Tiberius's brother **Gaius** wrote in a pamphlet that Tiberius, traveling through Etruria on his way to Spain, saw the countryside empty of free men, with only foreign slaves working the land and herding the flocks, and that this first inspired his policy (Plutarch, *Tiberius Gracchus* 8). Others said that he was stung by the senate's repudiation of the Numantine treaty that he had negotiated, or that he was encouraged by his teachers and his mother.

## The land law

In **133 BCE**, Tiberius was elected **tribune of the plebs**. He proposed a **land law** (*lex agraria*). It revived the old limit, said to go back to the Licinian-Sextian laws, on the amount of **public land** (*ager publicus*) that any individual could occupy: **500 iugera**, with an additional 250 for each of up to two sons, perhaps a total of 1,000. The land held in excess of this limit, which the rich had occupied, often for generations, treating it as their own, would be reclaimed by the state, and redistributed in small plots, of perhaps 30 iugera, to poor citizens, who would hold them as inalienable leases, so that they could not be bought up again by the rich. A board of three men (**triumviri**) would carry out the reform. The aim, according to Appian, was to restore a class of small farmers, and so to increase the number of men eligible for military service (*Civil Wars* 1.9–11).

Plutarch gives Tiberius a speech, in which he described the plight of the Roman poor:

> "The wild beasts that roam over Italy have their dens and lairs, but the men who fight and die for Italy share in the air and light, and nothing else. … They fight and die to protect the wealth and luxury of others, and they are called the masters of the world, but they have not a single clod of earth to call their own." (Plutarch, *Tiberius Gracchus* 9, plain rendering)

## The veto and the deposition

The proposal was popular with the poor, but bitterly opposed by the wealthy, who had occupied the public land for generations, invested in it, built on it, and buried their ancestors in it. Tiberius took his bill directly to the plebeian assembly, without first consulting the senate, which was legal but contrary to custom. The opponents of the bill persuaded another tribune, **Marcus Octavius**, to veto it.

Tiberius then took an unprecedented step. He proposed that the assembly vote to **depose** Octavius, on the grounds that a tribune who acted against the interests of the people was no longer a true tribune. The tribes voted one by one; when seventeen of the thirty-five had voted for deposition, Tiberius begged Octavius to withdraw his veto; he refused. The eighteenth tribe voted, and Octavius was removed from office, and dragged from the speaker's platform. The bill was passed, and a land commission was established, consisting of Tiberius, his brother Gaius, and his father-in-law, **Appius Claudius Pulcher**, the *princeps senatus*.

The deposition of Octavius shocked many Romans. It was a direct challenge to the principle of collegiality, and to the tribune's right of veto, which had been the protection of the plebs. If the majority of the assembly could remove any magistrate who opposed it, what limits remained on its power?

## Pergamum

The senate, which controlled the treasury, gave the land commission a tiny budget. At that moment, news arrived that **Attalus III**, king of **Pergamum**, had died and bequeathed his kingdom to the Roman people. Tiberius proposed that the king's treasure be used to fund the land commission, and that the people, not the senate, should decide how to organize the new territory. This was an intrusion into the senate's traditional control of finance and foreign affairs.

## The death of Tiberius

Tiberius's opponents accused him of aiming at **kingship**. A senator claimed that the envoy from Pergamum had brought Tiberius a royal diadem and a purple robe. Fearing prosecution when his year of office ended, Tiberius stood for re-election as tribune for the following year, which was without recent precedent, and perhaps illegal.

On the day of the election, in the summer of 133, the assembly met on the Capitol. In the senate, which was meeting nearby in the temple of Fides, the consul **Publius Mucius Scaevola**, a distinguished lawyer, refused to use force against a citizen without trial. Then **Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio**, the pontifex maximus, and Tiberius's own cousin, cried, "Let those who wish to save the state follow me," pulled the hem of his toga over his head, as a priest at a sacrifice, and led a band of senators and their followers, armed with clubs and the legs of broken benches, to the Capitol. Tiberius and some three hundred of his supporters were clubbed to death. Their bodies were thrown into the Tiber (Plutarch, *Tiberius Gracchus* 18–20; Appian, *Civil Wars* 1.14–16).

## The beginning of the end

The death of Tiberius Gracchus was, as Plutarch and Appian both saw, a turning point. Appian wrote: "This was the first conflict in Rome to end in bloodshed and the death of citizens; … after this, there was never any lasting peace in the Republic" (*Civil Wars* 1.2, plain rendering). For the first time, a political dispute had been settled by violence in the streets of Rome. The land commission continued its work for some years, and seems to have distributed land to many thousands of citizens; boundary stones set up by the commissioners, inscribed with their names, have been found in southern Italy. But the precedent of political violence had been set.

When Scipio Aemilianus, returning from the destruction of Numantia, was asked what he thought of his brother-in-law's death, he quoted Homer: "So perish whoever else would do such deeds" (*Odyssey* 1.47). He died suddenly in 129, in circumstances that some suspected were murder.

> **Pause and consider.** Tiberius Gracchus deposed a fellow tribune who vetoed his law, and was killed by senators who believed he aimed at tyranny. Who, in your judgment, broke the rules of the Republic first?`,
