      body: `## The younger brother

Ten years after his brother's death, **Gaius Sempronius Gracchus** was elected tribune for **123 BCE**, and again for **122**, re-election having been made legal in the meantime. He was, according to the ancient sources, a more passionate and powerful orator than his brother: he was the first, Plutarch says, to walk about on the speaker's platform as he spoke, and to pull his toga off his shoulder, and he kept a slave behind him with a pitch pipe, who sounded a note to calm him when his voice grew too loud and angry (Plutarch, *Tiberius Gracchus* 2). Cicero considered him one of the greatest orators of Rome.

Gaius's program was far broader than his brother's. In two years, he passed a series of laws that touched almost every aspect of Roman life:

- He renewed and extended his brother's **land law**, and founded **colonies**, including one on the site of Carthage, called **Junonia**, the first Roman colony outside Italy.
- He passed a **grain law** (*lex frumentaria*), providing that the state would sell grain to citizens in Rome at a fixed, subsidized price, and built public granaries. This was the beginning of the grain dole, which later became free and was one of the defining features of the city of Rome.
- He passed a law requiring the state to provide clothing for soldiers without deducting it from their pay, and forbidding the enlistment of boys under seventeen.
- He built **roads** throughout Italy, creating employment.
- He reaffirmed the right of **appeal** (*provocatio*), declaring that no citizen could be put to death without the judgment of the people, and making a magistrate who did so liable to prosecution, a law aimed at those who had killed his brother.
- He arranged for the taxes of the new province of **Asia** (the former kingdom of Pergamum) to be collected by companies of **equites**, the **publicani**, who bid for the contracts in Rome. This proved a disaster for the provincials, who were exploited by the tax collectors, but it gave the equites a powerful stake in the empire.
- Most important politically, he transferred the juries of the **extortion court** from the senators to the **equites**. Now senators accused of extortion as governors would be judged by the businessmen whose interests in the provinces they might have opposed. Gaius is said to have boasted that he had thrown daggers into the Forum, with which the senators and the equites would wound each other (Cicero, *On the Laws* 3.20).

## Citizenship for the allies

Gaius's most controversial proposal, in **122 BCE**, was to extend Roman **citizenship** to the **Latins**, and Latin rights to the other Italian allies. The Italians had fought in Rome's wars for centuries, and bore the burdens of empire, but had little share in its benefits; they had also been affected by the land law, which reclaimed public land that some of them occupied. But the proposal was unpopular with the Roman people, who did not wish to share their privileges, such as cheap grain and seats at the games. The consul **Gaius Fannius**, who had been elected with Gaius's support, turned against him, and asked the people whether they thought that, once the Latins were citizens, there would be room for them at the assemblies and festivals (*ORF* Fannius fr. 3, quoted by Julius Victor). The senate's ally, the tribune **Marcus Livius Drusus**, outbid Gaius with even more generous proposals, which were never carried out. Gaius's citizenship proposal failed, and he was not re-elected for a third term.

## The senatus consultum ultimum

In **121 BCE**, the consul **Lucius Opimius**, a bitter enemy of Gaius, moved to repeal some of his laws, including the colony at Carthage. During a public sacrifice, one of the consul's attendants was killed by Gaius's supporters. The senate responded by passing, for the first time, a decree that became known as the **senatus consultum ultimum**, the "ultimate decree of the senate," which urged the consuls "to see to it that the state suffer no harm" (*videant consules ne quid res publica detrimenti capiat*). The decree was understood as authorizing the consuls to use force, without regard to the normal protections of citizens, against those who threatened the state.

Opimius armed the senators and the equites, and summoned a force of Cretan archers. Gaius and his supporters occupied the **Aventine**, the traditional refuge of the plebs. Gaius, according to Plutarch, refused to fight, and fled across the Tiber to a grove sacred to the Furies, where he ordered his slave to kill him. Opimius had promised to pay for Gaius's head its weight in gold; the man who brought it was said to have removed the brain and filled the skull with lead (Plutarch, *Gaius Gracchus* 17). Some three thousand of Gaius's supporters were killed or executed without trial. Opimius then restored the temple of **Concord** in the Forum; someone wrote beneath the dedication, by night: "A work of discord makes a temple of Concord" (Plutarch, *Gaius Gracchus* 17).

## Cornelia

Cornelia, the mother of the Gracchi, outlived both her sons. She retired to her villa at Misenum, on the Bay of Naples, where she entertained Greek scholars and visitors from abroad, and spoke of her sons, Plutarch says, without tears, as of men of an earlier age (*Gaius Gracchus* 19). Two fragments of letters attributed to her, urging Gaius not to seek the tribunate and harm the state, are preserved by the biographer Cornelius Nepos, though their authenticity is debated. After her death, the Roman people set up a statue of her, inscribed "Cornelia, daughter of Africanus, mother of the Gracchi." The base of a statue with a similar inscription survives.

## Optimates and populares

The careers of the Gracchi gave rise to a new kind of politics. Roman writers, especially Cicero, came to distinguish between two kinds of politician: the **optimates**, "the best men," who championed the authority of the senate, and the **populares**, "men of the people," who appealed directly to the assemblies, often through the tribunate, with proposals for land, grain, citizenship, and debt relief. These were not organized parties, with programs and members, like modern political parties; they were styles of politics, and most populares were themselves members of the nobility. Many of them, as Sallust observed, used the name of the people to pursue their own power (*Catiline* 38). But the division between them would shape the politics of the rest of the Republic.

The Gracchi themselves became symbols. To the optimates, they were dangerous demagogues who aimed at tyranny. To the populares, they were martyrs for the people. In the 1790s, the French revolutionary **François-Noël Babeuf**, an early advocate of communal property, took the name "**Gracchus**."

> **Pause and consider.** The senate's "ultimate decree" told the consuls to see that "the state suffer no harm," and was used to justify killing citizens without trial. When, if ever, may a government suspend the normal protections of law to save itself?`,
